## Migration: `EventProcessor` → .NET 8 with Cooperative Cancellation

### Problems in the Original Code

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable for waking `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be interrupted cooperatively |

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (true)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call interruption | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` + `Thread.Abort()` in `Stop()` | Preemptive termination | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var waitHandles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny returns index of the signaled handle
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(waitHandles);

            if (signaled == 1) // CancellationToken was signaled
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // signals the token's WaitHandle, waking WaitAny
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation is the only option
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

1. **`WaitHandle.WaitAny` is essential** — a plain `_newEvent.WaitOne()` can't be woken by cancellation. You must include `_cts.Token.WaitHandle` in the array.

2. **Check queue count after wake** — a race exists where `Stop()` signals while the queue is empty. Always guard `Dequeue()`.

3. **No forcible kill exists** — if `Handle(item)` hangs indefinitely, `Stop()` will wait forever. Add `_cts.Token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` checkpoints inside `Handle` if it does long-running work, or move uncooperative work to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill`.

4. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — it holds timer/callback resources. Implement `IDisposable`.

5. **Lost signals** — the original code had a bug: if multiple items were enqueued before the loop woke, only one was dequeued. Consider draining the queue in a loop:

```csharp
// Drain all available items per wake-up
lock (_lock)
{
    while (_queue.Count > 0)
        Handle(_queue.Dequeue());
}
```

### Modern Alternative (Channel-based)

For new .NET 8 code, `System.Threading.Channels` eliminates manual synchronization entirely:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _worker;

    public void Start()
    {
        _worker = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);

    public async Task StopAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();
        _cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // timeout fallback
        if (_worker is not null)
            await _worker;
    }

    public void Dispose() => _cts.Dispose();
    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

This is the idiomatic .NET 8 approach — no locks, no `WaitHandle`, no manual threads.
